// pbrt's wavefront for volpath, on the CPU: a queue per kernel of a bounce,
// as pbrt's GPU renderer has them (apps/pbrt/PLAN.md, "pbrt's GPU
// wavefront, written as a schedule of vol_path_step").
//
// The program is written at pbrt's kernel boundaries -- vol_path_step is the
// trace kernel and the routing after it, vol_surface the material kernel,
// escaped_contribution and emissive_contribution the two radiance kernels,
// shadow_contribution the shadow kernel -- and every queue here is a call of
// one of them deferred where the program makes it. No `stage`: each boundary
// is a call in tail position or a spawned one, so a queue entry is the
// call's arguments and nothing else, and the drain runs the callee as
// written. No gang here: the drains are the plain loops, so that this is the
// structure checked against pbrt on its own before it is vectorized or put
// on the device. Every scene runs volpath under this schedule as under
// pbrt --gpu; a scene that names another integrator runs it un-deferred.
schedule {
    walk_step.loopify();
    path_step.loopify();
    full_path_step.loopify();
    // volpath's inner walks are loops within a step (see wavefront.bonsai).
    vol_medium_step.loopify();
    vol_shadow_step.loopify();
    vol_shadow_track.loopify();

    // First: a copy of the render per integrator, so that the queues below
    // are the volpath copy's alone -- the other integrators run as written
    // in their own copies, and the chain from render to vol_path_step is
    // one integrator's (pbrt --gpu is volpath and nothing else).
    render.specialize(integrator);

    // pbrt's ray queue (RayQueue, one per depth: the current and the next).
    // Its pushers are the camera ray (GenerateCameraRays: li_vol_path's call,
    // so nothing traces in the producer), the ray turned at a medium
    // scattering event (SampleMediumScattering: vol_path_step's call to
    // itself), and the material kernel's next ray -- the bounce's and the
    // interface skip's (EvaluateMaterialsAndBSDFs, PushIndirectRay: the two
    // calls in vol_surface). Its drain is the trace kernel and what pbrt
    // does with the intersection (TraceClosest, EnqueueWorkAfterIntersection),
    // which pushes the three queues after it.
    rays = render[VolPath].queue(p);
    li_vol_path.defer(vol_path_step, rays);
    vol_path_step.defer(vol_path_step, rays);
    vol_surface.defer(vol_path_step, rays);

    // pbrt's queues after the trace, drained in pbrt's order each round.
    // The escaped rays (escapedRayQueue, HandleEscapedRays) and the emissive
    // hits (hitAreaLightQueue, HandleEmissiveIntersection): spawned calls,
    // their values going into the path's radiance and nowhere else.
    escaped = render[VolPath].queue(p);
    vol_path_step.defer(escaped_contribution, escaped);
    emissive = render[VolPath].queue(p);
    vol_path_step.defer(emissive_contribution, emissive);
    // The hits, one queue per material type -- the interface skips, the
    // diffuse, the coated, ... -- each drained by a copy of the material
    // kernel for that material alone (pbrt's EvaluateMaterialAndBSDF<M>).
    hits = render[VolPath].queue(p);
    vol_path_step.defer(vol_surface, hits);
    hits.specialize(material);
    // pbrt's shadow ray queue. The shadow ray is spawned in vol_sample_ld
    // once the light is sampled -- `spawn l += shadow_contribution(...)` --
    // so its trace is pushed by the material drains and by the medium
    // scattering in the rays drain, and run after the last of them each
    // round, as pbrt's TraceShadowRays follows its material kernels. The
    // film write, which reads the radiance, then waits for every round, as
    // pbrt's UpdateFilm does: the compiler moves it onto a queue of its
    // own, drained after the rounds.
    shadow = render[VolPath].queue(p);
    vol_sample_ld.defer(shadow_contribution, shadow);
    // Not yet pbrt's: the rays that start in a medium do their medium walk
    // in the rays drain, where pbrt sends them to a medium-sample queue and
    // its scatter to another (SampleMediumInteraction, SampleMediumScattering)
    // and has both feed the material queues alongside the trace.

    trace.sort(primitives.Interior,
               |i : u8, r : Ray, axis : u8|
               select((1.0 / r.d)[axis] < 0.0,
                      1.0 - cast[[Float]](i), cast[[Float]](i)))
         .sort(Inst.blas.Interior,
               |i : u8, r : Ray, axis : u8|
               select((1.0 / r.d)[axis] < 0.0,
                      1.0 - cast[[Float]](i), cast[[Float]](i)))
         .loopify(64);
    trace_any.loopify(64);

    render.bind(p, CPUThread);
}
